### Step 4 — AI Schema Mapping

**Sub-heading:** "Step 4 of 8 — AI Generates Schema Mapping"

This is an automatic step. The user lands here and sees an animation immediately.

**What the user sees (while AI is working):**
- A card in the center: **"🤖 AI is analyzing your MongoDB schema…"**
- A subtle animated pulse/spinner
- Below the spinner, a text log that updates live:
  - *"Reading users collection... ✅"*
  - *"Reading orders collection... ✅"*
  - *"Sending schema to AI... ✅"*
  - *"Generating PostgreSQL table layout... ⏳"*

**What the user sees (once AI is done):**
- The loading area is replaced with the **Interactive Schema Mapper** — the main visual editing table.
- A small banner at the top: **"🤖 AI has suggested a mapping. Review it below and make any changes you want."**

**The Schema Mapper (the main interactive table):**

The mapper is divided by source collection. For each collection, there is a section heading and a table.

*Example — Collection: "users" → Table: "users"*

| MongoDB Field | Type | → | PostgreSQL Column | Data Type | Nullable | Include? |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `_id` | ObjectId | → | `id` | VARCHAR(24) ▼ | No | ✅ |
| `name` | String | → | `name` | VARCHAR(255) ▼ | No | ✅ |
| `email` | String (98%) | → | `email` | VARCHAR(255) ▼ | Yes | ✅ |
| `address.city` | String | → | `address_city` | VARCHAR(100) ▼ | Yes | ✅ |
| `address.zip` | String | → | `address_zip` | VARCHAR(20) ▼ | Yes | ✅ |
| `createdAt` | Date | → | `created_at` | TIMESTAMPTZ ▼ | Yes | ✅ |

**Interaction rules:**
- **Data Type dropdown (▼):** Clicking opens a dropdown where the user can switch the data type (e.g., change `VARCHAR(255)` to `TEXT`, or `INTEGER` to `BIGINT`)
- **Include checkbox:** Unchecking removes this field from the migration entirely. The row gets a strikethrough.
- **Column name:** The user can click on the PostgreSQL column name and type to rename it.
- Special badges appear next to fields:
  - `⚠️ Was Nested` — field was inside an embedded object, now flattened
  - `🔗 Foreign Key → users.id` — this field references another table
  - `📋 Creates child table: order_items` — this array field will become a separate table

*If a collection (e.g., "orders") has a field `items` that is an Array of Objects:*
- A special row appears: `items (Array of Objects) → Creates table: "order_items" [View Details]`
- Clicking "View Details" expands a sub-section showing the child table mapping

---

**📘 Data Type Reference Panel (collapsible, shown below the mapper):**

A collapsible section the user can open to see exactly how MongoDB types map to PostgreSQL types. This helps them make informed decisions when changing types in the dropdown.

| MongoDB (BSON) Type | Maps To (PostgreSQL) | Notes shown in UI |
|---|---|---|
| ObjectId (12-byte) | `VARCHAR(24)` | Use as string ID. Safe default. |
| String (UTF-8) | `TEXT` or `VARCHAR(N)` | TEXT is default. Use VARCHAR if you know the max length. |
| NumberInt (32-bit) | `INTEGER` | Direct 1:1 mapping. |
| NumberLong (64-bit) | `BIGINT` | Use BIGINT to avoid JavaScript precision loss on large numbers. |
| Double (float) | `DOUBLE PRECISION` | Direct IEEE 754 mapping. |
| Decimal128 | `NUMERIC(p, s)` | Preserves full precision. Never use DOUBLE for money. |
| ISODate / Date | `TIMESTAMPTZ` | Always use TIMESTAMPTZ (timezone-aware). Never plain TIMESTAMP. |
| Boolean | `BOOLEAN` | Direct mapping. |
| Binary (UUID subtype) | `UUID` | Converted to standard 36-char hyphenated UUID string. |
| Binary (generic) | `BYTEA` | Raw binary data. Stored as hex. |
| Array of Strings/Ints | `TEXT[]` or `INT[]` | Native PostgreSQL array type. |
| Array of Objects | Separate child table + FK | Or JSONB if structure is irregular. |
| Nested Object (1–2 levels) | Flattened columns | e.g. `address.city` → `address_city` |
| Nested Object (3+ levels) | `JSONB` | Too deep to flatten cleanly. Use JSONB with GIN index. |
| null / missing field | `NULL` column | Column must allow NULL. Never NOT NULL without a default. |
| GeoJSON Point | `JSONB` | Or PostGIS `geometry(Point,4326)` if extension is enabled. |

*This table is read-only reference information. Actual type choices are made in the mapper rows above.*

---

**🗂️ Index Translation Section (shown below the field mapper, one section per collection):**

After the column mapping table for each collection, there is a separate **"Indexes"** sub-section. The app reads all existing MongoDB indexes from the source database and proposes equivalent PostgreSQL indexes.

*Example — Indexes for Collection: "users"*

| MongoDB Index | Type | → | PostgreSQL Equivalent | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `{ email: 1 }` | Standard | → | `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_email ON users(email);` | B-Tree. Standard lookup. |
| `{ username: 1 }, unique: true` | Unique | → | `ALTER TABLE users ADD CONSTRAINT unique_username UNIQUE(username);` | Unique constraint. |
| `{ bio: "text" }` | Text Search | → | `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_bio_fts ON users USING GIN (to_tsvector('english', bio));` | Full-text search via GIN. |
| `{ status: 1, created_at: -1 }` | Compound | → | `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_status_created ON orders(status, created_at DESC);` | Compound B-Tree. Order matters. |
| `{ tags: 1 }` | Multikey (Array) | → | `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_tags ON products USING GIN(tags);` | GIN index for array columns. |

**All generated PostgreSQL index commands automatically include `CONCURRENTLY`** so that index creation never locks the table during migration.

**Include/Exclude toggle:** Each proposed index has a checkbox so the user can exclude any index they don't want to migrate.

**Special warning badge** appears on GIN indexes: *"⚠️ GIN indexes are fast to read but slower to update. If this column is updated very frequently, consider whether you need this index."*

